I was in grade school in the 60's when we all had to learn
the "new math", which was mostly learning how to do 
arithmetic in other bases besides base 10.  So there we sat
in 3rd grade learning how to add in base 2 and base 8 and
in 5th grade learning how to do long division in base 7 and
hexidecimal. 

Why?  Because computers worked in bases 2, 8 and 16 and we 
were all going to need to know this stuff to compete with
the Russians in the arms and space race.

The short-sighted part of this was that it wasn't very long
before the computers were doing the base-conversions for us.
Hardly anyone has to be facile with hexidecimal arithmetic
in the way they thought we would need.

How long will it be before we have software that easily 
translates between languages?

The real reason English won't go away is that it's the national
language of so many countries which were in the British Empire
that had many tribal units with their own languages.  Those
countries adopted English as their national language after
England left/got kick out since it was the only common tongue
among the units.  There are a whopping lot of countries using
English as their national language, the largest of which is
_India_.  (Also a growing progamming/technilogical powerhouse.)

By the way, an Italian pilot landing an Italian airplane
